<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the official Developer ID (notarized DMG) build.

  Deliberately empty, and that is the whole point of the file.

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime, which Tauri enables. The hardened
  runtime is a set of restrictions; every entitlement here is a hole punched
  back through it. A thin client needs none:

    - The window is a WKWebView. Its JavaScript JIT runs in a separate,
      Apple-signed WebContent process with its own entitlements, not in ours —
      so `allow-jit` and `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` buy nothing.
    - The binary is one statically linked Mach-O with no bundled dylibs, so
      library validation has nothing to reject.
    - Network access and Keychain access need no entitlement outside the App
      Sandbox, and this build is not sandboxed.

  This file used to carry four exceptions, including
  `disable-library-validation`, which permits *any* unsigned dylib to load into
  the process. They were correct when the bundle shipped a PyInstaller backend:
  an embedded CPython allocates writable-executable pages for ctypes closures
  and loads hundreds of unsigned .so files at runtime, and without them the
  interpreter died on launch. The official build stopped shipping that backend
  when it became a thin client, so the exceptions outlived their reason while
  still weakening every release that carried them — the sharpest of them
  cancelling out much of what signing is for.

  The self-host build still bundles the sidecar and still needs all four. They
  live in `Entitlements.selfhost.plist`, selected by
  `tauri.selfhost.conf.json`. If a future official build embeds an interpreter
  again, take them from there rather than writing them from memory.

  Keep this empty. An entitlement belongs here only with a named binary that
  fails without it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
